Feature: Promotion
  The [[challenger]] and the [[champion]] play the same 256 seeds. For each seed, the league
  subtracts the score of the champion from the score of the challenger. The challenger is
  better if the mean of these differences is more than 0, with 95 % confidence. When a
  generation has more challengers, each one must be surer, so that luck does not promote one of
  many.

  The games are noisy. The difference between two executors in one game is often 0.7 or more.
  With 256 games, the league can see a difference of about 0.1.

  A better challenger becomes the champion only if it is also safe. It must not crash. It must
  not leave the arena clearly more often than the champion: more than two standard deviations
  of chance (with 9 exits for the champion, 15 is too many). The [[safety envelope]] must not change its
  demands more than 2 times as often as for the champion. A chaser that follows its target to
  the edge of the arena makes the [[fence]] turn it back more often, so a better executor can
  need the envelope a little more.

  Expectation: A challenger that scores better on the same seeds becomes the champion
    Given the champion is the slow executor
    And the challenger is the default executor
    When the league judges the challenger
    Then the verdict is promoted

  Expectation: A challenger that scores worse stays out
    Given the champion is the default executor
    And the challenger is the slow executor
    When the league judges the challenger
    Then the verdict is kept out

  Expectation: A challenger that crashes stays out, whatever it scores
    A crash in the arena is a crash of a real plane later. No score makes up for it.

    Overrides: A challenger that scores better on the same seeds becomes the champion

    Given the champion is the slow executor
    And the challenger is the default executor
    And the crashes of the candidate are 1
    When the league judges the challenger
    Then the verdict is refused
    And the reason is it crashed
